{"source_key":"sha256-db92dd9d54884ccb80de5ab4e2691d5a","record_index":305,"record_hash":"sha256:6324ad472c294f081acfb7289b5c238fc6803d67eb4b7e3d28b08dd1ab2a0da2","timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:22:52.304Z","kind":"tool_result","source_event_kind":"codex:response_item:custom_tool_call_output","role":null,"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n\n---\nname: brainstorming\ndescription: Use before creative implementation work—creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior—when you need to explore intent, requirements, constraints, and design before touching code. For this workspace, adapt the flow to OpenClaw and local planning artifacts rather than OpenCode-specific tools.\n---\n\n# Brainstorming\n\nTurn ideas into clear designs and approved specs through collaborative dialogue.\n\n## Hard gate\n\nDo not implement, scaffold, write code, or invoke coding/execution skills until:\n1. you have explored the current project context,\n2. asked clarifying questions as needed,\n3. proposed approaches with tradeoffs,\n4. presented a design, and\n5. the user has approved the design.\n\nEven simple work gets a design pass. The design can be short when the task is small, but it still needs to exist.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Explore project context first\n   - Read relevant files, docs, and recent repo state.\n   - Follow existing patterns before proposing change.\n2. Ask clarifying questions one at a time\n   - Prefer multiple choice when helpful.\n   - Focus on purpose, constraints, success criteria, and scope.\n3. Propose 2–3 approaches\n   - Include tradeoffs.\n   - Lead with your recommendation and why.\n4. Present the design\n   - Scale detail to complexity.\n   - Cover architecture, components, data flow, failure modes, and testing where relevant.\n   - Confirm it looks right before moving on.\n5. Write the design down\n   - Save to `docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md` unless the repo already has a better local convention.\n6. Self-review the written design\n   - Remove placeholders.\n   - Fix contradictions.\n   - Tighten ambiguity.\n   - Ensure the scope is appropriate for one implementation plan.\n7. Ask the user to review the spec file\n   - Wait for approval or edits.\n8. Transition to planning\n   - Use the `writing-plans` skill next.\n\n## Scope discipline\n\nIf the request actually describes mu\n[TRUNCATED]\nxtremist. Deletion before addition. Ship the one-liner and challenge the rest of the requirement in the same breath. |\n\nExample: \"Add a cache for these API responses.\"\n- lite: \"Done, cache added. FYI: `functools.lru_cache` covers this in one line if you'd rather not own a cache class.\"\n- full: \"`@lru_cache(maxsize=1000)` on the fetch function. Skipped custom cache class, add when lru_cache measurably falls short.\"\n- ultra: \"No cache until a profiler says so. When it does: `@lru_cache`. A hand-rolled TTL cache class is a bug farm with a hit rate.\"\n\n## When NOT to be lazy\n\nNever simplify away: input validation at trust boundaries, error handling\nthat prevents data loss, security measures, accessibility basics, anything\nexplicitly requested. User insists on the full version → build it, no\nre-arguing.\n\nNever lazy about understanding the problem. The ladder shortens the\nsolution, never the reading. Trace the whole thing first — every file the\nchange touches, the actual flow — before picking a rung. Laziness that skips\ncomprehension to ship a small diff is the dangerous kind: it dresses up as\nefficiency and ships a confident wrong fix. Read fully, then be lazy.\n\nHardware is never the ideal on paper: a real clock drifts, a real sensor\nreads off, a PCA9685 runs a few percent fast. Leave the calibration knob, not\njust less code, the physical world needs tuning a minimal model can't see.\n\nLazy code without its check is unfinished. Non-trivial logic (a branch, a\nloop, a parser, a money/security path) leaves ONE runnable check behind, the\nsmallest thing that fails if the logic breaks: an `assert`-based\n`demo()`/`__main__` self-check or one small `test_*.py`. No frameworks, no\nfixtures, no per-function suites unless asked. Trivial one-liners need no\ntest, YAGNI applies to tests too.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nPonytail governs what you build, not how you talk (pair with Caveman for\nterse prose). \"stop ponytail\" / \"normal mode\": revert. Level persists until\nchanged or session end.\n\nThe shortest path to done is the right path.\n","tool_name":"exec","tool_kind":"other","tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:22:52.304Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","call_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]"}}}}